content:
**Term Life Insurance — the grace remittance.** The grace remittance that restores a policy in its grace period is the overdue billed premium, plus the newly due installment counted in the grace remittance when one has fallen due without being billed, plus the returned-item charge.

## I. Overdue billed premium

The total of every billed installment on the ledger, less every remittance applied, plus every payment returned unpaid by the bank.

## II. Newly due installment

**Count.** An installment has fallen due without being billed when a full month or more has passed since the latest billed due date and that latest billed installment had been drafted by monthly bank draft (the ledger shows a remittance by bank draft against that due date); exactly one such installment is then counted, and none is counted otherwise.

**Amount.** For the newly due installment:

- the insured's issue age is the whole years from the date of birth to the issue date on record; the anniversary age, the whole years from the date of birth to the most recent policy anniversary on or before today; the policy years completed, the whole years from the issue date to today;
- the band measurement face is the face amount on record plus both permanent face amounts when an in-force Ashgrove whole life policy and an in-force Ashgrove universal life policy are both on record for the insured, plus that one permanent face amount when exactly one of the two is in force for the insured, and the face amount on record alone otherwise; the band factor is 1.00;
- the face premium is the face amount on record divided by 1,000, times the rate per 1,000 on record, times the band factor, rounded to the cent;
- the fee before sharing is set by the face amount on record: the product's annual policy fee less 10.00 below 250000, less 20.00 at 1000000 or more, and the product's annual policy fee from 250000 up to 1000000; the long-standing fee concession is nothing; the policy fee share is the whole fee; the policy fee is the fee before sharing less the concession, never below nothing, times the share, rounded to the cent;
- the accidental death rider rate is the product's accidental death rider rate per 1,000; the accidental death charge is the rider amount on record divided by 1,000, times that rate, rounded to the cent;
- the children's term unit rate is 5.00 at 11 or more units on record, 5.50 at 5 through 10 units, and the product's children's term rate per unit below 5 units; the children's term charge is the units on record times that rate;
- the waiver rate is 0.14 when the issue age is 45 or more, 0.09 when it is below 35, and 0.11 when it is from 35 through 44; the waiver charge base is the face premium plus the accidental death charge plus the children's term charge at issue age 40 or more, the face premium plus the accidental death charge at 30 through 39, and the face premium alone below 30; when the waiver of premium rider is attached on record the waiver charge is the rate times the base, rounded to the cent, and otherwise there is none;
- the annual premium is the face premium plus the policy fee, the accidental death charge, the children's term charge and the waiver charge;
- the modal factor is that of the premium mode on record (the product's quarterly, monthly bank-draft, semiannual or annual factor);
- the newly due installment is the annual premium times the modal factor, rounded to the cent.

## III. Returned-item charge

The returned-item charge rate is nothing, and the items charged are every returned item of the 12 months ending today; the returned-item charge is the rate times the items charged.

## IV. The remittance and its application

The grace remittance is the overdue billed premium plus the newly due installment times the installments counted, plus the returned-item charge, rounded to the cent; the policy returns to in force when it is received no later than the last day of grace.

The grace remittance is entered as one payment record that pays every installment it covers, the overdue billed installments first and the newly due installment next, and the paid-to date moves forward by one modal period of the mode in which each covered installment falls due: the mode under which an overdue installment was billed, and the premium mode on record for the newly due installment. The part of a grace remittance that pays the newly due installment and the returned-item charge is applied with it and is never treated as unapplied money.

### Worked figure (hypothetical)

Ledger: installments of 52.40 billed for 10 February, 10 March and 10 April; drafts of 52.40 applied to 10 February and 10 March; the 10 March draft returned unpaid, so the paid-to date stands at 10 March. Overdue billed premium: 157.20 less 104.80 plus 52.40, which is 104.80. No remittance by draft is on the ledger against the latest billed due date of 10 April, so no installment is counted as fallen due without being billed; the returned-item charge is nothing. Grace remittance: 104.80. It covers the overdue installments of 10 March and 10 April, each billed monthly, so the paid-to date moves forward two monthly periods to 10 May.
